Analysis: Quantum computers currently function as specialized accelerators with minimal integration into production workflows. The overhead of quantum-classical communication often negates computational advantages for problems requiring fewer than 50 logical qubits.

2.2 Classical Computing's Scalability Limits

Von Neumann architecture faces fundamental bottlenecks:

· Memory Wall: Processing speed outpaces memory bandwidth by 10x annually
· Power Wall: Dennard scaling ended in 2006; power efficiency improvements slowed to 3%/year
· Reliability Wall: Failure rates increase exponentially with transistor density

Current solutions (caching, pipelining, speculation) provide diminishing returns with 20-30% performance improvement per generation versus historical 50-60%.

2.3 System Resilience Deficits

Traditional fault tolerance approaches (checkpoint-restart, replication, erasure coding) fail in three critical scenarios:

1. Cascading Failures: Single component failure triggers system collapse (exponential dependency)
2. Unknown Unknowns: Systems cannot respond to unanticipated failure modes
3. Performance-Reliability Tradeoff: High availability requires 200-300% resource overhead

The 2025 Cloud Infrastructure Failure Report documented 327 major outages despite 99.999% SLA guarantees, costing $1.2B in economic losses.

2.4 Energy Efficiency Crisis

Global data centers consume 1.5% of worldwide electricity (320 TWh annually), projected to reach 8% by 2030. Current efficiency measures face thermodynamic limits:

· Cooling represents 40% of data center energy use
· Idle servers consume 50-70% of peak power
· Quantum refrigeration (10-15 mK) requires 10kW per qubit

2.5 Security Vulnerabilities in Hybrid Systems

